why is the universe so damn quiet out  there  why is the universe in three dimensions  rather than ten  could we code a supercomputer into a  proton  the three body problem and its two  sequels the dark forest and death's end  try to answer progressively more bizarre  scientific questions  and i want to be clear i don't finish  series trilogies of books very often  but in doing so this became one of my  favorite science fiction series that i  have ever read session lou the author  for the three body problem won the hugo  award for best novel for this book  and let's look at why that was deserved  what do you think momo  do you think the three body problem  deserved it no no no you think  you think you want chin scratches there  we go  there we go oh  but first what's it about well the free  body problem is about  um it's about this group of people that  they  uh um okay  most importantly it's about ideas ideas  in the way that old science fiction  of navin and arthur c clarke and asimov  were a lot of the time it's about  complex ideas and the discussion about  them plot wise  it's kind of hard to describe without  giving everything away but  it's about humanity's first contact with  an alien civilization  except that they're not here yet  except that they kind of are and they  won't be here for hundreds of years  capiche okay it's a book about slowly  figuring out who these aliens are  why they're coming from all the way out  there and what we  should do or not do about it should we  talk with them  should we fight them if we were to fight  them how would we fight them who should  even be in charge in the first place or  should we just let them come  maybe it'll be great now you may love  this book for the same reason that a lot  of people  really hated it and if you look at  goodreads or amazon reviews then they  will mention  this it is packed full of dense  complicated  often bizarre scientific concepts and  seashell will just take  two pages to explain it to you it's  expositiony in many ways you might look  at it like that  but i found it compelling because it's  really cool ideas that he's talking  about and  the way that it works into the book is  quite brilliant at many times i mean not  just quite brilliant like it's  genuinely incredibly brilliant i just  used way too many adjectives  this book and the two sequels even more  so just went way further than most of  the hard science fiction that i'm used  to reading  and the way that xianlu did it was  really cool because  he sort of introduces a lot of concepts  that are very real that we  know work that aren't just theory like  super hard graphite materials or  space elevators which could conceivably  happen  even within our own lifetimes but then  he also introduces some really bizarre  speculative stuff you know like building  a  supercomputer into a proton  but he does it in a way that really  blurs the line between stuff that we  know is possible and is pretty  speculative that might not be that we're  not sure about it's kind of just there  in theory  and the way that he meshes them together  he convinces you that it's all possible  that this  is how things could work so it gives you  the simpler stuff and you  buy into the more bizarre stuff but at  the same time i want to be clear that  this is not a  character-driven novel and the  characters in the book  can feel pretty wooden at times this is  partly because they're often used as  representations for ideas more than  they're meant to be kind of their own  independent characters  and the fact that it was originally  written in chinese means that as it was  translated over  a lot of the dialogue the way people  talk doesn't  feel like the way people should talk to  english speakers  that's not a fault of the book but it  does affect how you might experience it  and engage with the characters  i didn't really feel like i needed that  deep character-driven stuff but if you  were the sort of person where you don't  like dense scientific concepts or you  really depend on those characters to get  immersed in a story  then this may not be the book for you  and that's okay it can be quite  abrupt if it's not what you're used to  like there's this moment in death's end  where he just says  and then she lost the ability to speak  for a few days and then she got it back  basically but stylistically i don't  think any of that really undermined any  of the real  draw for me to this story this  fascinating exploration of our place in  the universe  the actual way that it's written can  feel quite strange to an english speaker  as it was written in chinese there's  a lot of puns and there's a lot of  references that chinese readers would  understand but we don't they do actually  however give us some in the footnotes  which i always found  really interesting that like hey this  name means this or this is a reference  to this event from the chinese cultural  revolution  part of this is because of the chinese  cultural context of the book most of it  is told from the perspective of  chinese characters in chinese history  with a lot of references to  chinese culture and the history that  surrounds it  so some of that nuance is going to be  lost on  you as english speakers the writing  style can also feel  unfamiliar at points i mentioned how the  dialogue can feel wooden but also  transitions for example they might just  say and then one year passed which  feels strange and very abrupt for us  reading it but that's not on the book  that's not a stylistic problem with the  book that is all to do  with my experience as an english speaker  and  reader and i actually think that reading  this sort of stuff it's good to expand  what we can be familiar with um so  for me it was actually kind of  enlightening to read a book with  such a different way of telling stories  and building worlds and characters uh  even if they're not the way that i'm  used to  and meant that i didn't quite get into  