t up like this when  one is  clearly more fascinating than the other  but  seashell doesn't really suffer from this  at all there are so many  questions and answers in both of these  stories  and how they fit together because they  feel just  wildly disconnected for so long  uh and then how they come together it's  brilliant it genuinely is  amazing uh and it's such a unique way to  do it  that was one thing that really stuck out  to me is that you know the setting of  the chinese cultural revolution it's so  unlike anything that i'm used to in  science fiction and  seeing how that plays into quite  naturally  the events of the actual story and how  it leads up to it it's a mic drop moment  one thing to mention is that though the  three body problem does stand on its own  it's also pretty clearly written as a  set up book for the story that seashell  new  really wanted to tell in the dark forest  and death's end  and that's fine but it did mean that the  story ended in a place where i felt like  oh  so the story hasn't even started yet and  because of that  it is probably the weakest of the three  in the trilogy  and it's why the immediate sequel the  dark forest is easily the strongest of  the trilogy it's where the meat of that  story that i wanted to know about could  be found  and i don't actually have the dark  forest here because i lent it to a  friend after we read this  in less than a week  it's that good it's also the most  character-driven of the three he spends  a lot of time getting us  invested in the perspective character  and it has a strong  thematic central scientific question  that drives the plot  why is it so quiet out there  and it all builds together to a really  profound  ending but if you like speculative but  also hard science fiction  then get the three body problem and its  sequel  the dark forest that's all from me let  me know what you would like me to review  next  down in the comments below i have been  reading a lot  and if i haven't 