u it's  expositiony in many ways you might look  at it like that  but i found it compelling because it's  really cool ideas that he's talking  about and  the way that it works into the book is  quite brilliant at many times i mean not  just quite brilliant like it's  genuinely incredibly brilliant i just  used way too many adjectives  this book and the two sequels even more  so just went way further than most of  the hard science fiction that i'm used  to reading  and the way that xianlu did it was  really cool because  he sort of introduces a lot of concepts  that are very real that we  know work that aren't just theory like  super hard graphite materials or  space elevators which could conceivably  happen  even within our own lifetimes but then  he also introduces some really bizarre  speculative stuff you know like building  a  supercomputer into a proton  but he does it in a way that really  blurs the line between stuff that we  know is possible and is pretty  speculative that might not be that we're  not sure about it's kind of just there  in theory  and the way that he meshes them together  he convinces you that it's all possible  that this  is how things could work so it gives you  the simpler stuff and you  buy into the more bizarre stuff but at  the same time i want to be clear that  this is not a  character-driven novel and the  characters in the book  can feel pretty wooden at times this is  partly because they're often used as  representations for ideas more than  they're meant to be kind of their own  independent characters  and the fact that it was originally  written in chinese means that as it was  translated over  a lot of the dialogue the way people  talk doesn't  feel like the way people should talk to  english speakers  that's not a fault of the book but it  does affect how you might experience it  and engage with the characters  i didn't really feel like i needed that  deep character-driven stuff but if you  were the sort of person where you don't  like dense scientific concepts or you  really depend on those characters to get  immersed in a story  then this may not be the book for you  and that's okay it can be quite  abrupt if it's not what you're used to  like there's this moment in death's end  where he just says  and then she lost the ability to speak  for a few days and then she got it back  basically but stylistically i don't  think any of that really undermined any  of the real  draw for me to this story this  fascinating exploration of our place in  the universe  the actual way that it's written can  feel quite strange to an english speaker  as it was written in chinese there's  a lot of puns and there's a lot of  references that chinese readers would  understand but we don't they do actually  however give us some in the footnotes  which i always found  really interesting that like hey this  name means this or this is a reference  to this event from the chinese cultural  revolution  part of this is because of the chinese  cultural context of the book most of it  is told from the perspective of  chinese characters in chinese history  with a lot of references to  chinese culture and the history that  surrounds it  so some of that nuance is going to be  lost on  you as english speakers the writing  style can also feel  unfamiliar at points i mentioned how the  dialogue can feel wooden but also  transitions for example they might just  say and then one year passed which  feels strange and very abrupt for us  reading it but that's not on the book  that's not a stylistic problem with the  book that is all to do  with my experience as an english speaker  and  reader and i actually think that reading  this sort of stuff it's good to expand  what we can be familiar with um so  for me it was actually kind of  enlightening to read a book with  such a different way of telling stories  and building worlds and characters uh  even if they're not the way that i'm  used to  and meant that i didn't quite get into  the characters as much as someone else  might if i spoke the language  but the story and the series more  broadly does have a collectivist  bent to it and what i mean by that is  that in  in the sequel the dark forest i remember  this distinct moment where he writes  and then earth solved all of their  problems and decided to work together  and i was just like what  just just baffled uh but apparently  that sort of collectivist utopian ideal  is more common in uh in chinese fiction  whereas i'm a lot more used to  individualist fiction where there's  there's a lot more focus on individual  characters  the the movers of these stories of the  three-body problem the dark forest and  death scene  are organizations they're groups of  people much more than the individual  characters  western media does also have stories  that put more of an emphasis on the  community and the collective rather than  the individual  a star trek does this quite a bit but  apparently it is more common  in chinese fiction than we might have it  now i do want to give you a warning  because the three-body problem the first  book in the series  did feel weirdly  young adultish when i was first reading  it for the first kind of  part of it you know you've got all of  the world's smartest people  being invited to uh join a secret  society and solve this complicated  science problem that no one else can  called the three body problem and i  won't tell you how it fits into the  story because it's  a brilliant reveal but uh  it felt kind of younger in in how it was  uh  playing out but as it progresses it  definitely does become more complicated  and  more mature and that feeling definitely  went away for me  the book is divided up into a couple of  perspectives one  in the wake of the chinese cultural  revolution at a secret chinese military  base  and one in the present trying to figure  out the  consequences of what happened at that  base  now normally i'm always worried about  stories that split it up like this when  one is  clearly more fascinating than the other  but  seashell doesn't really suffer from this  at all there are so many  questions and answers in both of these  stories  and how they fit together because they  feel just  wildly disconnected for so long  uh and then how they come together it's  brilliant it genuinely is  amazing uh and it's such a unique way to  do it  that was one thing that really stuck out  to me is that you know the setting of  the chinese cultural revolution it's so  unlike anything that i'm used to in  science fiction and  seeing how that plays into quite  naturally  the events of the actual story and how  it leads up to it it's a mic drop moment  one thing to mention is that though the  three body problem does stand on its own  it's also pretty clearly written as a  set up book for the story that seashell  new  really wanted to tell in the dark forest  and death's end  and that's fine but it did mean that the  story ended in a place where i felt like  oh  so the story hasn't even started yet and  because of that  it is probably the weakest of the three  in the trilogy  and it's why the immediate sequel the  dark forest is easily the strongest of  the trilogy it's where the meat of that  story that i wanted to know about could  be found  and i don't actually have the dark  forest here because i lent it to a  friend after we read this  in less than a week  it's that good it's also the most  character-driven of the three he spends  a lot of time getting us  invested in the perspective character  and it has a strong  thematic central scientific question  that drives the plot  why is it so quiet out there  and it all builds together to a really  profound  ending but if you like speculative but  also hard science fiction  then get the three body problem and its  sequel  the dark forest that's all from me let  me know what you would like me to review  next  down in the comments below i have been  reading a lot  and if i haven't 